#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105577 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/id #uuid "2eb23241-bd2b-5b76-b885-25cd3a51c16d" :question/math-academy-id "e-392" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=(2x-4)^2+2$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $\\Delta x=0.5$." :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/94/94618262fb303035cd03ccf0274694d7083d9cd3a23d4231e3a8fccde8ac4fe1.png)\n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $11$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx (11 + 6 + 3 + 2) \\cdot 0.5; = 22 \\cdot 0.5; = 11\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105578 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/id #uuid "41e80d7f-8a72-5bbf-9f66-c0047b28e45b" :question/math-academy-id "e-394" :question/problem "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[1,4]$.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8.5$ | $10$ | $8$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the four subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,4]$." :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=4$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is computed as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx f(2)\\Delta x_1+f(3)\\Delta x_2+f(3.5)\\Delta x_3+f(4)\\Delta x_4\n$$\n\nwhere each $\\Delta x_i$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\Delta x_i = x_{i}-x_{i-1}\n$$\n\nWe set up a table of values that include the $\\Delta x$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\Delta x$ | $-$ | $1$ | $1$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $8.5$ | $10$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve, and get\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx 4 \\cdot 1 + 8.5 \\cdot 1 + 10 \\cdot 0.5 + 8 \\cdot 0.5; = 4 + 8.5 + 5 + 4; = 21.5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105648 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/id #uuid "c1deb898-8696-537f-8fb2-ae0f354dec29" :question/math-academy-id "e-7231" :question/problem "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-4,2]$. The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $12$. What must be the value of $k$?\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $5$ | $3$ | $k$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula for the right Riemann sum:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(-2)}}+{\\color{red}{f(0)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $k$ | $2$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx (3 + k + 2) \\cdot 2; = (k + 5) \\cdot 2; = 2k + 10\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $\\mathcal A$ must be $12$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2k+10 & =12 \\\\[5pt] 2k &= 12-10 \\\\[5pt] k &= \\dfrac{2}{2} \\\\[5pt] k &= 1. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105649 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/id #uuid "1f74cbf1-c3f5-5ac2-ad55-ad22d7dae3ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-7233" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=(2x-4)^2+2$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $\\Delta x=0.5$. Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/94/94618262fb303035cd03ccf0274694d7083d9cd3a23d4231e3a8fccde8ac4fe1.png)\n\nThe area $\\mathcal{A}$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\mathcal A\\approx(\\overbrace{{\\color{red}{f(0.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1)}}+{\\color{red}{f(1.5)}}+{\\color{red}{f(2)}}}^{\\color{red}{\\large\\text{heights}}})\\cdot \\underbrace{\\color{blue}\\Delta x}_{\\color{blue}{\\large\\text{width}}}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $11$ | $6$ | $3$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx (11 + 6 + 3 + 2) \\cdot 0.5; = 22 \\cdot 0.5; = 11\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[0,2]$, the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bd814a6-30d6-59ac-a780-e4809fcd45e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a672268875752204a3f8332a1761cc0482687b5469c5b9ddd45044e8e0fa2b39.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104363} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b95a77-626a-5888-945f-ead2bb6eb3b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4d4e96c9dc96dcfc8ef6c2fe984a915ff047531ce33706dd781a9d159ca82950.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104364} {:answer/id #uuid "2925a444-5ec3-524b-bca1-b952f071d65c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/19/1929cc971dec72740c30825bae06c5119b8055da6d4f2bd37e9654bb84542b39.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104365} {:answer/id #uuid "3529a846-a5aa-50fd-8996-d967d0623533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1a3070d66463de310537f519a992a6357908597e2661249a72a11cf6582d019c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104366} {:answer/id #uuid "54d038e6-6f3a-5bf7-a195-1d9bbd720b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d525a78713b4f34c4fda8e2ac8e74a665691fd114a033f7727b91435bf5124fb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104367}] :answer-field/id #uuid "87dde1bd-f911-5689-afee-6d9b1969b89c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186104362}] :question/id #uuid "aa1b07db-8546-5dcc-a57c-3d4bbe55271b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49117" :question/problem "A right Riemann sum is used to approximate the area under the curve $y = f(x)$ between $x =-3$ and $x =-1$ for each of the functions below. For which function does the Riemann sum give an *overestimate* of the area?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e0e2534-557c-5d1d-a4c1-a63a3979c0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105059} {:answer/id #uuid "a5dd965c-513e-5c45-be86-386b4fde7bdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105060} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f8f7a3-d0f4-5000-b70c-55ece98dc00f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105061} {:answer/id #uuid "e569548f-e5e4-5a19-a049-83ace359cba6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105062} {:answer/id #uuid "59352711-4b84-54f5-8f21-16affdcef33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105063}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d9bbd5d1-52fc-5532-9c11-a0ca2391cb50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105058}] :question/id #uuid "7d9f3e81-c696-5e30-a1a6-32de40a197d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74324" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y = 4 - 2x$ over the interval $[0, 1]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx = 0.25$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342196 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105722 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1649648b-d221-5e0a-8f9d-630907a81603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339324} {:answer/id #uuid "173114fb-816a-522c-8b10-d7a5c6b86cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32.55" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339334} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb672c4-690f-5f25-a8a4-44bb1e8ddbd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "34.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340087} {:answer/id #uuid "a83683d8-5ae3-5945-b911-de4f9feb8c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340654} {:answer/id #uuid "d77d48dc-cd96-56f7-a04a-d30082752caf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a83683d8-5ae3-5945-b911-de4f9feb8c00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340654} :answer-field/id #uuid "1347de4e-e87f-5553-b355-971c6eb6c1a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc8544c2-610f-58ff-bd57-2903f678a6bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-74348" :question/problem "The following table shows the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum, with the three subintervals indicated by the data, to approximate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[4,9].$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have $n=3$ rectangles. Using the right Riemann sum, the area is approximated as \n\n$$\nA≈f(5.5)Δ{x}_{1}+f(8)Δ{x}_{2}+f(9)Δ{x}_{3},\n$$\n\nwhere each $Δ{x}_{i}$ is calculated by considering the step between a particular $x$-value and the previous one, i.e.,\n\n$$\nΔ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}.\n$$\n\nWe set up a value table that includes the $Δx$'s.\n\n| $x$ | $4$ | $5.5$ | $8$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $Δx$ | $-$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $1$ |\n| $f(x)$ | $-$ | $4$ | $6$ | $9.5$ |\n\nFinally, we plug in the numbers to approximate the area under the curve and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈4\\cdot 1.5+6\\cdot 2.5+9.5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =6+15+9.5 \\\\  & =30.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
